1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,625
Assembled in Singapore: The
Short Life of Local Car Plants

2
00:00:04,625 --> 00:00:09,147
Since early 2023, Hyundai has
been building electric cars in a

3
00:00:09,227 --> 00:00:14,269
seven-storey plant in the Jurong
Innovation District — the first time

4
00:00:14,349 --> 00:00:19,025
cars have been assembled in
Singapore in more than forty years.

5
00:00:19,025 --> 00:00:23,003
That gap is easy to read as
the natural state of things:

6
00:00:23,083 --> 00:00:27,567
Singapore is small, has no car
industry, taxes cars harder than

7
00:00:27,647 --> 00:00:31,850
almost anywhere on earth, and
imports every one it drives.

8
00:00:31,850 --> 00:00:35,975
For about half a century,
though, it did assemble them.

9
00:00:35,975 --> 00:00:39,059
The plants were never
large and never lasted,

10
00:00:39,139 --> 00:00:43,348
and the last of them shut in
1980, but for a while "assembled

11
00:00:43,428 --> 00:00:47,225
in Singapore" was a label
you could find on a new car.

12
00:00:47,225 --> 00:00:49,750
The story starts with Ford.

13
00:00:49,750 --> 00:00:54,194
The Ford Motor Company of Malaya
was incorporated in November

14
00:00:54,274 --> 00:00:57,827
1926 to run Ford's business
across Malaya, Burma,

15
00:00:57,907 --> 00:01:02,721
Thailand and the Dutch East Indies
from Singapore, and it opened a

16
00:01:02,801 --> 00:01:07,325
plant that same year in a rented
shophouse off Enggor Street.

17
00:01:07,325 --> 00:01:10,185
Calling it an assembly plant is generous:

18
00:01:10,265 --> 00:01:13,985
the main jobs were fitting
wheels and touching up the

19
00:01:14,065 --> 00:01:17,650
paint on Model T Fords
that arrived largely built.

20
00:01:17,650 --> 00:01:21,563
In 1929 it moved to a warehouse
on Prince Edward Road,

21
00:01:21,643 --> 00:01:28,514
and from 1930 it began putting together
semi-knocked-down cars — kits of major sub-assemblies,

22
00:01:28,594 --> 00:01:31,620
shipped flat and bolted together locally —

23
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:36,950
from Ford's Canadian and British
factories, for sale around the region.

24
00:01:36,950 --> 00:01:39,875
Ford did well out of Malaya.

25
00:01:39,875 --> 00:01:45,097
Its mass-produced cars undercut
British and European makes on price,

26
00:01:45,177 --> 00:01:48,996
and by 1939, with war
cutting off European supply,

27
00:01:49,076 --> 00:01:52,975
Ford held about 80 per
cent of the Malayan market.

28
00:01:52,975 --> 00:01:56,862
In 1941 the company opened
a purpose-built plant on

29
00:01:56,942 --> 00:02:00,441
Upper Bukit Timah Road,
designed by the French

30
00:02:00,521 --> 00:02:04,953
engineer-architect Émile Brizay
in a clean Art Deco style,

31
00:02:05,033 --> 00:02:09,000
with three tall green-glazed
windows down one wing.

32
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,424
It was the first full
motor-vehicle assembly plant

33
00:02:12,504 --> 00:02:15,507
in Southeast Asia, and
it started doing body

34
00:02:15,587 --> 00:02:18,950
assembly rather than just
bolting kits together.

35
00:02:18,950 --> 00:02:23,350
The plant's most famous day
had nothing to do with cars.

36
00:02:23,350 --> 00:02:29,052
On the 15th of February 1942, ten weeks
after the Japanese landed in Malaya,

37
00:02:29,132 --> 00:02:32,475
Lieutenant-General Arthur
Percival signed the

38
00:02:32,555 --> 00:02:37,500
surrender of Singapore at a table
inside the Bukit Timah factory.

39
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:40,933
Under the occupation the
Japanese army used the

40
00:02:41,013 --> 00:02:44,750
building to assemble trucks
under the Nissan name.

41
00:02:44,750 --> 00:02:48,996
Ford got it back in 1946,
resumed car assembly in 1947,

42
00:02:49,076 --> 00:02:53,086
and kept the line running
for another three decades,

43
00:02:53,166 --> 00:02:57,727
taking completely-knocked-down
kits from the United States,

44
00:02:57,807 --> 00:03:00,875
Canada, Britain, Germany and Australia.

45
00:03:00,875 --> 00:03:05,561
By the time it closed in June
1980 it had built more than

46
00:03:05,641 --> 00:03:11,075
150,000 vehicles and grown from
sixteen workers to three hundred.

47
00:03:11,075 --> 00:03:17,314
Ford's Bukit Timah plant was the only
car assembler in Singapore until 1965,

48
00:03:17,394 --> 00:03:21,800
and that is largely because
the action was next door.

49
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:25,787
Through the early 1960s,
governments across the region were

50
00:03:25,867 --> 00:03:30,129
pushing import-substitution
industrialisation — the idea that a

51
00:03:30,209 --> 00:03:34,196
country should make at home
what it had been buying abroad,

52
00:03:34,276 --> 00:03:38,825
behind a wall of tariffs high
enough to make local production pay.

53
00:03:38,825 --> 00:03:41,625
Cars were an obvious target.

54
00:03:41,625 --> 00:03:45,848
In 1963 the Malaysian government
invited manufacturers to set

55
00:03:45,928 --> 00:03:50,151
up assembly plants, and by the
middle of the decade a cluster

56
00:03:50,231 --> 00:03:53,467
of them was going up
around Johor and Selangor,

57
00:03:53,547 --> 00:03:58,758
assembling everything from Austins and
Fords to the first Mitsubishi Colts,

58
00:03:58,838 --> 00:04:03,000
with talk of a home-grown
"national car" to follow one day.

59
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:06,492
Singapore was part of
Malaysia for two of those

60
00:04:06,572 --> 00:04:09,912
years and separate from
it after August 1965,

61
00:04:09,992 --> 00:04:14,400
and either way it was on the
wrong side of the arithmetic.

62
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:18,508
A national assembly industry
needs a domestic market big

63
00:04:18,588 --> 00:04:23,075
enough to give a plant some
scale, and Singapore's was tiny.

64
00:04:23,075 --> 00:04:25,903
The plants that mattered
were built where the

65
00:04:25,983 --> 00:04:28,682
customers and the
local-content rules were,

66
00:04:28,762 --> 00:04:31,800
which meant the Malaysian
side of the Causeway.

67
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:36,395
Kuala Lumpur's push eventually
produced Proton in 1983;

68
00:04:36,475 --> 00:04:39,875
Singapore's produced nothing comparable.

69
00:04:39,875 --> 00:04:43,675
It did, though, get a few
assembly lines of its own.

70
00:04:43,675 --> 00:04:48,195
Cycle & Carriage opened what
was described as Singapore's

71
00:04:48,275 --> 00:04:51,745
second car plant at
Hillview Avenue in 1965,

72
00:04:51,825 --> 00:04:56,910
screwing together Mercedes-Benz
saloons — mostly 200s and 280s —

73
00:04:56,990 --> 00:05:02,800
along with Daihatsu and Isuzu trucks
and the chassis for Mercedes buses.

74
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:07,316
By the 1970s there were four
assemblers running in Singapore:

75
00:05:07,396 --> 00:05:11,084
Ford at Bukit Timah, Cycle
& Carriage at Hillview,

76
00:05:11,164 --> 00:05:13,500
and lines for Nissan and Volvo.

77
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:16,257
All of them existed for the same reason:

78
00:05:16,337 --> 00:05:20,370
a tariff on fully-built imported
cars that made it cheaper

79
00:05:20,450 --> 00:05:23,925
to ship the cars in as
kits and finish them here.

80
00:05:23,925 --> 00:05:26,569
The tariff was the whole business model,

81
00:05:26,649 --> 00:05:30,600
and Singapore's government had
decided against that model.

82
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:35,127
After separation, the Economic
Development Board built its industrial

83
00:05:35,207 --> 00:05:39,333
strategy around exporting to the
world rather than protecting a

84
00:05:39,413 --> 00:05:42,872
home market — electronics,
oil refining, ship repair,

85
00:05:42,952 --> 00:05:48,213
later precision engineering — industries
where a small, open economy with a good

86
00:05:48,293 --> 00:05:52,900
port and a disciplined workforce
could compete without a tariff wall.

87
00:05:52,900 --> 00:05:56,582
Propping up half a dozen
sub-scale car lines with

88
00:05:56,662 --> 00:06:00,500
duties on imports was the
opposite kind of policy.

89
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:02,900
So the protection came off.

90
00:06:02,900 --> 00:06:05,955
In 1980 the government withdrew the tariff

91
00:06:06,035 --> 00:06:09,911
advantage for locally
assembled cars, and with it the

92
00:06:09,991 --> 00:06:13,350
reason those cars were
assembled here at all.

93
00:06:13,350 --> 00:06:17,378
Within the year, all four
plants — Ford, Cycle & Carriage,

94
00:06:17,458 --> 00:06:20,150
Nissan and Volvo — stopped assembling.

95
00:06:20,150 --> 00:06:23,300
Ford left Singapore altogether.

96
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:26,660
Cycle & Carriage turned
its Hillview plant into a

97
00:06:26,740 --> 00:06:30,099
respray and service centre,
and when the area was

98
00:06:30,179 --> 00:06:33,900
rezoned for housing, sold
the land for terrace homes.

99
00:06:33,900 --> 00:06:38,049
The Bukit Timah Ford building
went to a property group,

100
00:06:38,129 --> 00:06:42,278
lost half its floor area to
a condominium in the 1990s,

101
00:06:42,358 --> 00:06:45,814
and survived as a monument
only because of the

102
00:06:45,894 --> 00:06:50,200
seventy minutes in 1942 when
Percival sat at that table.

103
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:54,175
For the next four decades
Singapore made no cars.

104
00:06:54,175 --> 00:06:57,599
The motor trade became a
trade in the literal sense:

105
00:06:57,679 --> 00:07:02,248
importing finished vehicles,
distributing them, servicing them, and —

106
00:07:02,328 --> 00:07:06,493
as ownership was deliberately
capped through the Certificate of

107
00:07:06,573 --> 00:07:10,750
Entitlement system from 1990 —
reselling the right to own one.

108
00:07:10,750 --> 00:07:15,174
A new car in Singapore is among
the most expensive in the world,

109
00:07:15,254 --> 00:07:20,250
but essentially none of that money
is paid to anyone who built it here.

110
00:07:20,250 --> 00:07:23,750
The selling side left
better buildings behind than

111
00:07:23,830 --> 00:07:27,625
the making side did, though
not always as themselves.

112
00:07:27,625 --> 00:07:32,102
Malayan Motors, a Wearne Brothers
company — the same family

113
00:07:32,182 --> 00:07:36,505
that had been Ford's first
Singapore agent back in 1911 —

114
00:07:36,585 --> 00:07:41,835
ran a Swan & Maclaren-designed
showroom at 14 Orchard Road from 1927,

115
00:07:41,915 --> 00:07:46,550
with Rover, Rolls-Royce, Morris
and Studebaker on the floor.

116
00:07:46,550 --> 00:07:50,729
It made its last sale there in
August 1980, the same month the

117
00:07:50,809 --> 00:07:55,674
assembly plants lost their protection,
and the building went on to serve

118
00:07:55,754 --> 00:08:00,150
as an industry association's
offices and then a private college.

119
00:08:00,150 --> 00:08:04,683
The 1928 shophouse block
immediately beside it sold furniture

120
00:08:04,763 --> 00:08:08,540
and radios rather than
cars, but it is the one that

121
00:08:08,620 --> 00:08:12,623
was given a full heritage
restoration: acquired by the

122
00:08:12,703 --> 00:08:16,253
state and reopened in
2019 as Temasek Shophouse,

123
00:08:16,333 --> 00:08:21,400
a philanthropy hub with a café and
event spaces open to the public.

124
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:24,102
Hyundai's Innovation Centre Singapore,

125
00:08:24,182 --> 00:08:28,128
which opened in the Jurong
Innovation District in 2023,

126
00:08:28,208 --> 00:08:32,600
is a deliberate exception, and
a deliberately different one.

127
00:08:32,600 --> 00:08:37,540
It can build around 30,000 electric
vehicles a year — the Ioniq 5,

128
00:08:37,620 --> 00:08:42,788
including a driverless robotaxi
version, with the Ioniq 6 to follow —

129
00:08:42,868 --> 00:08:46,975
which is small by the standards
of a normal car plant.

130
00:08:46,975 --> 00:08:51,947
It is heavily automated, organised
into flexible assembly "cells"

131
00:08:52,027 --> 00:08:57,931
rather than a single moving line, run
against a digital simulation of itself,

132
00:08:58,011 --> 00:09:01,975
and topped with a 618-metre
test track on the roof.

133
00:09:01,975 --> 00:09:05,724
Hyundai and the government
describe it less as a factory

134
00:09:05,804 --> 00:09:09,975
than as a place to work out how
cars might be made in future.

135
00:09:09,975 --> 00:09:12,575
That framing is the point.

136
00:09:12,575 --> 00:09:16,550
The assembly plants of the
1960s and 70s were low-skill

137
00:09:16,630 --> 00:09:19,868
lines kept alive by a
tax on the alternative;

138
00:09:19,948 --> 00:09:22,818
they were exactly the kind of protected,

139
00:09:22,898 --> 00:09:28,575
labour-intensive work Singapore spent
the following decades moving away from.

140
00:09:28,575 --> 00:09:33,396
The Jurong plant is capital-intensive,
automation-heavy and pitched

141
00:09:33,476 --> 00:09:38,450
as research — the kind of manufacturing
Singapore now says it wants.

142
00:09:38,450 --> 00:09:42,136
Cars are being built in
Singapore again, but on terms

143
00:09:42,216 --> 00:09:46,125
almost opposite to the ones
that applied the last time.

144
00:09:46,125 --> 00:09:50,049
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Local car assembly is

145
00:09:50,129 --> 00:09:54,275
a neat measure of how Singapore
has chosen to make things.

146
00:09:54,275 --> 00:09:57,775
It flourished briefly when
a tariff made it worthwhile,

147
00:09:57,855 --> 00:10:01,680
ended the moment the government
decided tariffs were not how

148
00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:05,520
a small trading economy should
grow, and has come back only

149
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:08,644
in a form — small,
automated, sold as research —

150
00:10:08,724 --> 00:10:13,150
that matches the kind of industry
the country now wants its name on.
